window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-33","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-33-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-01/","title_he":"א – כבולעו כך פולטו","title_en":"As It Absorbs, So It Expels","body_en":"<p>As we have learned (above 32:1), the fundamental rule in the laws of kashering utensils is: <i>ke-bol'o kakh polto</i> (“as it absorbs, so it expels”) – the way a utensil was used with a forbidden substance determines the way it is kashered. There are three types of kashering: a) rinsing; b) <i>hagala</i> (purging in boiling water);<br>c) <i>libun</i> (heating to incandescence in fire). Let us explain further: the first type is rinsing in water, which is the lightest kashering, intended for utensils used with cold forbidden foods; to kasher them, one must rinse them in water and clean them of the remnants of the forbidden food that remained stuck to their walls. The second type is <i>hagala</i> in boiling water, intended for utensils in which forbidden foods were cooked. The word <i>hagala</i> denotes expulsion, for putting the utensil into boiling water makes it expel the flavors that were absorbed and stuck to it through cooking. The third type is <i>libun</i> in <i>ur</i> (fire), intended for utensils used directly in fire, such as skewers and grilling grates.<sup>1</sup></p><p>The difference between the three types of kashering is as follows. When utensils were used cold, the remnants of the foods stick to the utensils externally, and rinsing washes them off and kashers them. When utensils were used for cooking, the cooking causes the flavors of the foods to stick to and be absorbed in the utensils more strongly, so rinsing will not help them, and only <i>hagala</i> in boiling water, which melts the fats and expels the flavors that stuck to and were absorbed in the utensil, can kasher them. And when utensils were used in fire (<i>ur</i>), for roasting or baking, the flavors stick to and are absorbed in the utensils with great force and in a harder manner than in cooking, because cooking is done with liquids that soften the absorption and sticking of the flavors. In addition, cooking is generally done at a heat that does not exceed one hundred degrees, because the liquids in the cooking moderate the rise in heat, whereas baking and roasting, which are done without liquids, are done at higher heat. Therefore <i>hagala</i> in boiling water can expel only part of the flavors that stuck to and were absorbed in the utensil through fire, and to eliminate them one must perform <i>libun</i> on the utensil in fire, which will burn and consume the flavors absorbed in the baking pan or the skewer. It emerges that, as a rule, <i>hagala</i> expels and removes the flavor stuck to and absorbed in the utensil, whereas <i>libun</i> burns the flavor while it is still in it.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Mishna (Avoda Zara 75b): “One who acquires serving utensils from a gentile: those whose manner is … to be purged [in boiling water] – he purges them; to be heated to incandescence in fire – he heats them in fire. The skewer and the grill (<i>askala</i>, a roasting grate) – he heats them to incandescence in fire. The knife – he polishes it (scours it) and it is pure.” An elaboration appears in the baraita there: “Our Rabbis taught: One who acquires serving utensils from a gentile – items that were not used – he immerses them and they are pure (in accordance with the law of immersing utensils explained above in chapter 31). Items that were used with cold, such as cups, jugs (pitchers) and flasks – he rinses them and immerses them and they are pure; items that were used with hot, such as cauldrons (large pots), kettles and water heaters – he purges them and immerses them and they are pure; items that were used with fire, such as skewers and grills – he heats them to incandescence and immerses them and they are pure ….”"}]},{"slug":"17-33-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-02/","title_he":"ב – מדרגות ההגעלה","title_en":"The Levels of Hagala","body_en":"<p>The obligation of <i>hagala</i> follows the level of use of the utensil; there are four levels: a) a <i>keli rishon</i> (primary vessel) on the fire; b) a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire;<br>c) <i>irui</i> (pouring) from a <i>keli rishon</i>; d) a <i>keli sheni</i> (secondary vessel).</p><p><b>A <i>keli rishon</i></b> is the pot in which one cooks on the fire, as well as a spoon with which one stirs a dish cooking on the fire. If the dish was non-kosher, they are kashered the way they absorbed – in boiling water in a <i>keli rishon</i> on the fire.</p><p><b>A <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire</b> is a pot that was on the fire and was removed from it, or under which the fire was turned off. Since it is no longer being heated on the fire, its heat weakens and it lacks the power to cook and cause flavors to be absorbed as when it was on the fire. Therefore, if a spoon was put into it, the spoon can be kashered in a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire, provided that the water in it is still very hot.</p><p><b><i>Irui</i></b>: A bowl into which a non-kosher dish was poured from a <i>keli rishon</i> – since the non-kosher dish has been disconnected from the pot in which it was heated, it cannot cause flavors to be absorbed and to stick to the same degree as a <i>keli rishon</i>, and therefore it suffices to kasher the bowl by pouring very hot water over it from a <i>keli rishon</i>.</p><p><b>A <i>keli sheni</i></b> is the vessel into which the dish was poured from the <i>keli rishon</i>. If, for example, boiling water was poured into a <i>keli sheni</i>, and non-kosher meat was put into it to be heated in that water, the vessel requires kashering at the level of a <i>keli sheni</i>. Admittedly, many are lenient and hold that a <i>keli sheni</i> does not absorb and does not require kashering; however, since there are those who are stringent, one should perform <i>hagala</i> on a <i>keli sheni</i> as well.</p><p>A dish in a <i>keli rishon</i> that cooled down until its heat fell below 45 degrees cannot cook and cause flavors to be absorbed; therefore, if a spoon was put into it, the spoon does not require <i>hagala</i>, and it suffices to kasher it by rinsing.</p><p>Non-kosher dishes that are in a <i>keli shelishi</i> (tertiary vessel), even though they are very hot, since they have already moved far from the source of heat, are not considered to cause flavors to be absorbed or expelled; therefore the utensils that came into contact with them do not require kashering by <i>hagala</i>, and it suffices to kasher them by rinsing.<sup>2</sup></p><p>A knife with which one cut boiling non-kosher meat on the fire requires <i>libun</i>. If one cut the meat after it was removed from the fire, the knife requires <i>hagala</i>. Likewise, a fork stuck into a non-kosher baked item while it was in the oven requires <i>libun</i>; if after it was taken out of the oven, it requires <i>hagala</i>.</p><p>Regarding cold non-kosher soup that remained in a utensil for a full day, some say the utensil requires <i>hagala</i>, based on the rule that <i>kavush ke-mevushal</i> (“soaked is like cooked”). And this is the practice to rule <i>le-khatḥila</i> (ab initio). In time of need, one may be lenient and kasher glass and metal utensils by rinsing, since it is known that they do not absorb (below 35:9; Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 10:13).</p><p>Many are accustomed <i>le-khatḥila</i> to kasher all the utensils that require <i>hagala</i> in a <i>keli rishon</i>, that is, in boiling water on the fire, because they are concerned that perhaps the utensils were used in a <i>keli rishon</i> and this was forgotten (see Rema OḤ 451:6; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 107). However, when the level of the forbidden use is clear, there is no need to kasher the utensil at a higher level.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Some are stringent regarding a <i>keli shelishi</i> when the dish in it is very hot (Pri Ḥadash), but according to most poskim the law of a <i>keli shelishi</i> is like cold, and it suffices to kasher it by rinsing. This is because all the rules the Sages established are distant boundaries for extra safety, since generally even in a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire flavors are not absorbed, all the more so with <i>irui</i>, and all the more so in a <i>keli sheni</i> (and this is in addition to the basic rule that ordinary utensils are not <i>benei yoman</i> [used within the past twenty-four hours], and the flavor in them is spoiled and does not render food forbidden). Nevertheless, since in remote cases there may be some absorption, the Sages established safety rules and required that they be kashered; but one should not extend the concerns beyond the safety rules that were established. And this is the practice – not to kasher utensils that were used in a <i>keli shelishi</i>, all the more so when the utensils are of glass or metal (above 32:8). Regarding a very hot solid piece (<i>gush</i>), see below 35:4, where there is a dispute regarding a situation in which it is in a third or fourth vessel, etc. In practice, with metal and glass utensils, since they do not absorb and can be cleaned well, one should rule according to the lenient opinion. And with utensils that soak up flavors, such as wooden utensils, <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should take into account the opinion of those who are stringent regarding a <i>gush</i>."}]},{"slug":"17-33-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-03/","title_he":"ג – כלי שהשתמשו בו ברמות שונות","title_en":"A Utensil Used at Different Levels","body_en":"<p>The poskim disagree about how to kasher a utensil that was used with non-kosher food at different levels of use, such as a fork that is usually used with cold foods that are not hot enough to scald the hand (<i>yad soledet bo</i>), whose kashering is by rinsing, but is sometimes used with scalding-hot dishes, whose kashering is by <i>hagala</i>, and rarely is used for roasting, whose kashering is by <i>libun</i>.</p><p>Some say that it must be kashered according to its most severe use, for since it absorbed at a severe level of absorption, it must be kashered at that level. And some say that it must be kashered according to its majority use, and one need not be concerned about the severe absorption, because in practice after a full day the flavor absorbed in the utensil is already spoiled and cannot render foods forbidden. Nevertheless, one must still kasher a utensil that was used with non-kosher food, and the obligation is determined by the majority of the forbidden uses made of it.</p><p><i>Le-khatḥila</i> one should be stringent and kasher the utensil according to its most severe use; and in time of need, when it is difficult to kasher the utensil according to its most severe use, one may be lenient and kasher it according to its majority use. For example, a utensil whose kashering according to its most severe use would require <i>libun</i>, which might damage it, may be kashered by <i>hagala</i> according to its majority use.<sup>3</sup></p><p>Majority and minority in this matter are determined only according to the forbidden uses. For example, a fork with which one usually eats non-kosher food cold, and once stirred non-kosher food with it in a <i>keli rishon</i> – its kashering follows its majority use, by rinsing. But if it is a fork with which one always eats kosher foods cold, and once stirred non-kosher food with it in a <i>keli rishon</i> on the fire – its kashering is by <i>hagala</i> in a <i>keli rishon</i>, since only the use in the <i>keli rishon</i> was a forbidden use that requires kashering.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The reasoning of those who are stringent is clear: since the utensil once underwent a severe absorption, it must be kashered from it. This is the opinion of the She'iltot, Ra'avya, Mordekhai, Hagahot Maimoniyot, Terumat Ha-deshen, Issur Ve-heter He-arokh and Maharil. On the other hand, the Talmud implies that one follows the majority use of the utensil, and therefore the kashering of cups whose regular use is cold is by rinsing (Avoda Zara 75b), even though they are sometimes used with hot drinks; and the kashering of knives whose regular use is with boiling foods is by <i>hagala</i> (Pesaḥim 30b), even though meat is sometimes cut with them on the fire. This is also the view of Rabbeinu Tam, Ramban, Rashba, Ra'ah and Ran. And many understood the Rif and Rambam this way, as cited in Beit Yosef 451:6. Against the question of how kashering according to majority use can help for its severe absorption, they explained that after a full day the flavor is already spoiled and does not render forbidden, and therefore the Sages established that the kashering should follow the utensil's principal use (Responsa Rashba 1:372; Rama Mi-Fano 96; Gan Ha-melekh 53). According to this, one must be careful to kasher the utensils a full day after their severe use. This is also the view of Pri Ḥadash 451:6; Olat Shabbat 7; Ḥok Yaakov 31; Eliyahu Rabba 37; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 11; MB 46. But there is a difficulty, for most of the Rishonim who hold this view did not write that one must wait a full day from the severe use. Nahar Shalom 451:8 explained that in their opinion, even within a full day one may kasher according to majority use, since in practice even with this kashering the main flavor comes out. Thus far his words. Apparently his intent is that the rules of absorption and kashering established by the Sages are distant boundaries intended to encompass even remote cases, and there is no need to be stringent beyond these boundaries for very remote cases. And it seems that nowadays, with glass and metal utensils that were properly cleaned with soap, by the letter of the law, even when kashering according to majority use, there is no need to wait a full day from the severe use.<br>Simply, it seems that the Sephardic custom is to be lenient and kasher according to majority use, and the Ashkenazic custom is to be stringent according to the most severe use, as explained in SA and Rema OḤ 451:6; 25. Indeed, many Sephardic poskim wrote as a principle that one follows the majority use (SA 451:6; 25; Rama Mi-Fano, Gan Ha-melekh, Pe'ulat Tzaddik, Ziv'ḥei Tzedek, Rabbi Yosef Mashash, Yabi'a Omer vol. 10 YD 58; Or Le-Tziyon vol. 3 10:10-11). However, several Sephardic poskim wrote that <i>le-khatḥila</i> the custom is to be stringent according to the most severe use (Knesset Ha-gedola, Shulḥan Gavoha, Kaf Ha-ḥayim 451:100; Rav Ḥayim David Halevi, Mekor Ḥayim 186:1). And even the SA OḤ 451:20 wrote that the custom is to kasher tables by <i>irui</i> according to their most severe use. Conversely, the Rema OḤ 451:6 also wrote that <i>be-di'avad</i> (after the fact) one may kasher according to majority use. And it appears from Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 144 that the intent is that when it is difficult to kasher according to the most severe use, one may kasher according to majority use. Likewise, if kashering according to the most severe use could damage the utensil, according to Beit Meir 11 one may kasher it according to majority use, and Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 51 cites him. Since we find among Sephardim poskim who ruled to be stringent <i>le-khatḥila</i>, and among Ashkenazim poskim who ruled to be lenient in time of need, in order not to multiply disputes I wrote according to the opinion of the decisors who rule to be stringent <i>le-khatḥila</i> and lenient in time of need. Therefore, forks that are sometimes used in fire should be kashered by <i>hagala</i>, since they are liable to be damaged by <i>libun</i>. An additional reason: generally the fork that was used is nullified in the majority of forks that were not used in fire."},{"n":4,"en":"This is the opinion of Pri Megadim YD 91, Siftei Da'at 3; Beit Efrayim ḤM 17; Ḥazon Ish OḤ 119:15; Shevet Ha-levi vol. 6 116:3. However, in the glosses of Rabbi Barukh Frankel to siman 91, he is lenient and holds that the majority use is determined according to all its uses, including the permitted uses."}]},{"slug":"17-33-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-04/","title_he":"ד – הכנת הכלים להגעלה והכשרת סירים","title_en":"Preparing Utensils for Hagala, and Kashering Pots","body_en":"<p>One must clean the utensil before kashering it by <i>hagala</i>, because the boiling water removes the flavors absorbed in and stuck to the utensil, but does not clean the utensil of all the food remnants stuck to it. One who kashered a utensil by <i>hagala</i> without first cleaning it must clean it and perform <i>hagala</i> again (SA OḤ 451:3).</p><p>The handles of utensils must also be kashered, because in metal utensils, when the body of the utensil is heated, the heat also spreads to its handles, and if they reach a heat of <i>yad soledet bo</i>, the entire utensil, including its handles, is considered a utensil that was used with a forbidden substance, and all of it requires kashering. Wooden handles must also be kashered, for although they do not get so hot, sometimes the dish boils over or splatters onto them and its flavor is absorbed in and sticks to them (SA OḤ 451:12; MB 68). However, since the use and absorption of the handles is not as severe as that of a <i>keli rishon</i> on the fire, they can be kashered by <i>irui</i> from a <i>keli rishon</i> (Rema ibid.). When the handles of pots can be removed, some are <i>mehadrin</i> (meticulous) to remove them and clean them before the <i>hagala</i>. Instead, one may clean around them with plenty of soap, until it is clear that the food remnants that may have remained there have been spoiled.</p><p>Likewise, a utensil that has grooves from which it is difficult to remove food remnants must be cleaned with plenty of soap, until it is clear that the food remnants in the grooves have been spoiled, and afterwards one performs <i>hagala</i> on the utensil.</p><p>The <i>hagala</i> of a pot must be carried out inside a large vessel into which it can be entirely inserted. One should not suffice with boiling water inside the pot, because it is reasonable to assume that over the many times it was used, the dish boiled over or splattered from it onto the rim of the utensil, and then the forbidden flavor was absorbed in and stuck to the upper rim of the pot as well, and the rim of the pot is not kashered by boiling water inside the pot. When there is no large vessel in which the pot can be kashered, one should fill the pot one wishes to kasher with water and bring it to a boil, and at the same time boil water in a small vessel; when the water inside the pot boils, one puts the small vessel into it, and this will cause a lot of water to overflow and spill over the sides of the pot and kasher its rim (see Avoda Zara 76a; and see SA 452:6).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Some have suggested causing the water to overflow by pouring boiling water into it from a kettle. Indeed, <i>be-di'avad</i> this is effective, but the advice I wrote, to use a small vessel containing boiling water, is preferable, because with pouring the water cools slightly, whereas when the small boiling vessel is put into the large pot, the water does not cool at all, and the entire kashering is at the heat of a <i>keli rishon</i> on the fire.<br>Kashering a pot by causing a large overflow from it is effective for a utensil that absorbed by way of overflow, but a utensil that is usually inserted into another vessel, such as a ladle – its absorption is inside a <i>keli rishon</i>, and <i>hagala</i> by way of overflow will not help it; rather, one must perform <i>hagala</i> on the whole of it inside boiling water (MB 452:31). Pots that have a metal fold at their bottom rim do not require special treatment (Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 10, note 17, and in the 5780 edition 10, note 12)."}]},{"slug":"17-33-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-05/","title_he":"ה – המתנה עשרים וארבע שעות לפני ההגעלה","title_en":"Waiting Twenty-Four Hours Before Hagala","body_en":"<p>The custom is not to perform <i>hagala</i> on utensils that are <i>benei yoman</i>, that is, within twenty-four hours of their being used with a forbidden substance. This is because during a full day the flavor absorbed in and stuck to the utensil is still good, and if the <i>hagala</i> water does not contain sixty times the thickness of the utensil's walls, the water might take on the forbidden flavor and cause it to stick to and be absorbed in the utensil's walls again, and it would turn out that the <i>hagala</i> was ineffective. But after a full day, the flavor that was absorbed in and stuck to the utensil has become spoiled, and then even if the <i>hagala</i> water does not contain sixty times the utensil, the utensil will be kashered, because then the utensil expels a spoiled flavor into the water, and even if it reabsorbs from it, it does not become forbidden. For only if the flavor that was originally absorbed was good does the utensil remain in its forbidden state even after the flavor becomes spoiled; but if at the time the flavor was absorbed in and stuck to the utensil it was spoiled – the utensil does not become forbidden.</p><p>There is a further concern: if meat and dairy utensils are kashered in the same water, a good meat flavor and a good dairy flavor will be expelled into the <i>hagala</i> water, and if the <i>hagala</i> water does not contain sixty times more than one of them, the flavors will not be nullified in sixty and will mix with each other and render forbidden the <i>hagala</i> water and the utensils kashered in it, because they will absorb meat and dairy flavor together. But after a full day, the meat and dairy flavors expelled from the utensils will be spoiled and will not render the <i>hagala</i> water and the utensils forbidden, because <i>noten ta'am li-fgam</i> (a flavor that imparts a detrimental taste) is permitted (SA OḤ 452:2, MB ibid.).</p><p>In public <i>hagala</i> stations, the custom is not to rely on those coming to kasher to have left their utensils for a full day before the <i>hagala</i>, and in order to avoid mishaps, liquid soap is added to the <i>hagala</i> water; thereby any flavor expelled from the utensils is immediately spoiled, and even if it is reabsorbed in the utensils, since it is spoiled it does not render the utensils forbidden. Likewise, when one wishes to kasher utensils by <i>hagala</i> without waiting a full day, one may put liquid soap into the water and kasher them <i>le-khatḥila</i> within a full day.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"According to Rashi, the Eshkol, the Yere'im, Ramban and others, throughout the time the utensils are in the boiling <i>hagala</i> water, they expel flavor and do not absorb from the <i>hagala</i> water, and consequently there is no room for all the concerns cited above. However, Tosafot, Or Zaru'a, Rosh, Rashba, Semak and others are concerned about everything cited above, and this is the custom. This is also what SA and Rema OḤ 452:1-2 and MB 1 wrote. It emerges that one who wishes to kasher a utensil by <i>hagala</i> must be careful about one of two conditions: either a full day should pass over the utensils so that the flavors expelled from them will be spoiled (or one should spoil the <i>hagala</i> water with soap); or the <i>hagala</i> water should contain sixty times the thickness of the utensil's walls, in which case the expelled flavor is nullified in sixty.<br>However, according to Issur Ve-heter He-arokh, if utensils were put into the <i>hagala</i> water one after another, <i>kamma kamma batel</i> (each one in turn is nullified) in the water, and all the utensils are not combined together. But according to the Rashba, <i>ḥozer ve-ni'or</i> (the nullified substance is “reawakened”), and if the <i>hagala</i> water does not contain sixty times all the utensils put into it, one must be concerned about everything cited above. And this is the practice (MB 13). And the custom is even <i>le-khatḥila</i> not to kasher utensils that are <i>benei yoman</i>, lest people err and the <i>hagala</i> water not contain sixty times (MB 20).<br>All these principles applied also to old metal utensils that were not cleaned well with soap and water, as was customary until recent generations; but nowadays, when metal utensils are smooth and are cleaned with soap, and there is no concern that a layer of food remnants remained on them – there is no concern that they will expel flavor into the <i>hagala</i> water. Only with earthenware and wooden utensils is there a concern that they will expel flavors (above 32:4-8). Nevertheless, it is accepted practice to instruct, in public <i>hagala</i>, to mix soap into the <i>hagala</i> water, because this is necessary for wooden and plastic utensils, and because there are people who do not clean their metal and glass utensils properly.<br>Some hold that <i>hagala</i> can be done only with water, because other liquids do not properly expel flavors (Ramban, Ritva and Meiri). And some hold that <i>hagala</i> can be done with other liquids as well (Rashba). The Rema OḤ 452:5 ruled that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should perform <i>hagala</i> with water, and <i>be-di'avad</i> other liquids are also acceptable. Accordingly, several Aḥaronim wrote that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one should not mix ashes into the <i>hagala</i> water (Magen Avraham, Pri Ḥadash and Ḥok Yaakov). However, the accepted instruction nowadays is to mix soap into the <i>hagala</i> water, because the soap does not change the consistency of the water (and so wrote Sefer Ha-kashrut [?] 7, note 76)."}]},{"slug":"17-33-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-06/","title_he":"ו – הגעלת הכלים בפועל","title_en":"Performing the Hagala in Practice","body_en":"<p>Any of the kitchen pots may be used for the <i>hagala</i>, provided that it is clean. The <i>hagala</i> water must be boiling. In time of need, when soap is mixed into the <i>hagala</i> water, one may even boil the <i>hagala</i> water in a non-kosher pot, for since the flavor of the water is spoiled, the non-kosher utensil does not render the <i>hagala</i> water forbidden (although the pot itself has not been kashered, as explained above, section 4).</p><p>One leaves the utensil in the boiling water for about three seconds. <i>Le-khatḥila</i>, after the <i>hagala</i> the custom is to rinse the utensil in cold water, but this is not indispensable. Therefore, when it is difficult to rinse in cold water – there is no obligation to take the trouble to do so (SA OḤ 452:7; MB 34).<sup>7</sup></p><p>Sometimes putting the utensils in cools the water slightly until it stops boiling, and then one must leave the utensils in the water until it returns to a boil; the sign of this is that it produces bubbles.</p><p>A utensil that cannot be put entirely into the water may be kashered first one half and afterwards the other half (SA 451:11).</p><p>When one must kasher many utensils, such as cutlery, it is convenient to put them in a basket into the boiling water, and after leaving them for about three seconds, to take the basket out of the water, and the utensils are kosher. Since there is a concern that the utensils will be pressed against each other and the boiling water will not pass between them, one should shake the basket a little in the water so that the boiling water enters between the utensils and kashers them (based on SA OḤ 452:3-4).</p><p>One may kasher utensils without a basket by dropping the utensil into the boiling water, for while the utensil sinks in the boiling water, the water surrounds it on all sides and kashers it. If one needs to kasher several utensils, such as cutlery, one should drop them into the water one after another. If one wishes to fulfill the custom of rinsing them in cold water immediately after their kashering, one should pour out the boiling water and drop the kashered utensils into a vessel of cold water. However, as we have learned, there is no obligation to do so, and one may wait until the boiling water cools and then take out of it the utensils that have already been kashered.</p><p>In summary, this is the order of <i>hagala</i>: one cleans the utensil, boils water in a large, clean vessel, puts the utensil requiring <i>hagala</i> into the boiling water, sees that the water continues to boil, and after about three seconds takes out the kashered utensil. The custom is to add soapy water to the <i>hagala</i> water or to wait a full day between the forbidden use and the <i>hagala</i>. <i>Le-khatḥila</i>, one rinses the utensil in cold water after the <i>hagala</i>.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"All agree that the <i>hagala</i> water is intended to expel the flavor that stuck to and was absorbed in the utensils, for by being boiling it melts the fats that make the food remnants stick to the wall of the utensil, and expels the flavors. Many Rishonim wrote that one must leave the utensils in the <i>hagala</i> water until they expel their absorption into the water (Rif; Rambam, Hilkhot Ḥametz U-matza 5:23; Ra'avad, Ramban, Rashba, Ran and others). The Ra'avya and the Tur (452:1) wrote that the custom is to put the utensils in and take them out immediately. The Aḥaronim explained that this is so that they will not reabsorb what they expelled (MB 452:4). The Pri Ḥadash 452:6 explained that the custom is literally to put them in and take them out immediately, unlike most Rishonim, for the custom of Israel is Torah. On the other hand, the Taz and Eliyahu Rabba wrote that even according to the Tur and the custom, one must leave them in a little. And Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 452:4 explained that the intent is “to leave it in a very little bit.” It seems to me that their intent is about three seconds.<br>The Rishonim further wrote that after the <i>hagala</i> one must rinse the utensil in cold water, in order to remove from the utensils what they expelled, and so that the heat of the water will not cause the flavors expelled in the <i>hagala</i> to stick to and be absorbed in the utensils again (Rambam, Ra'avya, Rashba and many others. The Meiri wrote that instead of rinsing in cold water one may wipe the utensil in order to clean off the expelled matter stuck to its surface). However, the Rishonim wrote that rinsing in cold water is not indispensable, since it is not mentioned in the Talmud. The Aḥaronim explained that this is because the custom is to kasher utensils when they are not <i>benei yoman</i> (see MB 452:34)."}]},{"slug":"17-33-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-07/","title_he":"ז – ליבון","title_en":"Libun","body_en":"<p>Utensils whose use is in <i>ur</i> (fire), such as skewers and baking pans, are kashered by <i>libun</i>. It makes no difference whether the heat source is actual fire or electric heating coils and the like; whenever forbidden food was roasted or baked in them, they must be kashered by <i>libun</i>.</p><p>The Sages said that <i>libun</i> is putting the utensil into fire until “its peel falls off” (Bavli Avoda Zara 76a), or until sparks come out of it (Yerushalmi Avoda Zara 5:15). Since generally the iron itself does not peel and does not emit sparks in fire, it seems that the Sages' intent is that the <i>libun</i> will cause the foods stuck to the utensil to peel off it, or sparks to fly from them. The question is at what heat the utensil reaches this.</p><p>Many hold that the <i>libun</i> must be at a heat that burns and makes the metal glow, until all the food remnants that stuck to and were absorbed in the utensil are burned. This heat is about 350 degrees to about 400 degrees. And even though the fire on which the forbidden food was roasted or baked did not reach such a heat, in their opinion only when the utensil glows in the fire will all the forbidden flavors that stuck to and were absorbed in it be burned.</p><p>Some are lenient and hold that the rule <i>ke-bol'o kakh polto</i> applies to the heat of the fire as well, so that at the same heat at which the forbidden flavor stuck to and was absorbed in the utensil, it can be kashered. If its use was at a heat of 200 degrees – it can be kashered at 200 degrees. In their opinion, a baking pan in which forbidden food was baked in an oven can be kashered at the heat of that same oven.</p><p>In practice, the prevalent custom follows the stringent opinion, and since the pan is liable to be damaged by such <i>libun</i>, one should not kasher a baking pan. For this is the rule: any utensil that is liable to be damaged by kashering is not kashered, lest people be negligent in kashering it so as not to damage it (see above 32:4). However, in a case of great loss one may be lenient and perform <i>libun</i> at the heat at which the forbidden substance was used, in accordance with the lenient opinion.</p><p>Therefore, one should not kasher baking pans in which non-kosher food was baked, since severe <i>libun</i> is liable to ruin them. But one who has great difficulty buying new pans, either because their cost is high for him or because it is hard for him to obtain them, may kasher them by heating them in that same oven at the highest heat for about half an hour. The same applies to a <i>sir pele</i> (“wonder pot”) and a Teflon frying pan.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Concerning <i>libun</i>, it is stated in Bavli Avoda Zara 76a: “until their peel falls off,” and so it is cited in the She'iltot, Halakhot Gedolot, Rif, Rambam and others. And in Yerushalmi Avoda Zara 5:15: “And <i>libun</i> requires that sparks fly from it,” and so wrote the Rosh, Tur and SA 451:4. However, there is no dispute in this; rather, these are two signs of <i>libun</i>, and so wrote Pri Megadim 452, Mishbetzot Zahav 1. Seemingly the sparks and the peeling should be in the metal itself; however, metals generally do not emit sparks and also do not peel because of fire, and according to experts this was the case in the past as well, for even primitive metals do not emit sparks or peel. Therefore it seems that the intent is that the food remnants that stuck to and were absorbed in the walls of the utensils would burn and char and peel off, or burst and emit sparks. Rabbi Pfeuffer explained it along these lines (Derekh Kokhav p. 310), and his words are cited in many books (Ma'adanei Asher, Issur Ve-heter 159; Hora'a Berura YD 121:58; Divrei David vol. 1 YD 10).<br>As cited above, the Aḥaronim disagree regarding <i>libun</i>. Many hold that it refers to a fixed heat that produces sparks, among them: Pri Megadim (451, Eshel Avraham 30); Maharam Schick OḤ 213; Mikra'ei Kodesh of Rav Frank (Pesaḥ vol. 1 80:7); Igrot Moshe YD 1:60. And some were lenient to perform <i>libun</i> at the same heat at which the forbidden substance was used: Responsa Arugot Ha-bosem OḤ 119; Minḥat Yitzḥak 3:66; Tiferet Tzvi 1:30; and so wrote Nit'ei Gavriel (Pesaḥ 75:3) in the name of Rabbi Aharon Kotler, and Rabbi Yitzḥak Ze'ev of Brisk. In practice, it is accepted to rule according to the stringent opinion, whose words are quoted as halakha in the books of our time (Or Le-Tziyon vol. 3 10:2; Be-ohala Shel Torah 1:18; Sefer Ha-kashrut [?] 8:40; Hag'alat Kelim 5:6; 13:315).<br>According to Rabbi Avigdor, one of the Rishonim (cited in Hagahot Maimoniyot, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Assurot 17:5), there is a more lenient opinion: <i>libun</i> is kindling a fire under the utensil until, if straw were placed on it, it would be singed, and this is the Sages' intent with “until sparks fly from it.” In practice, it was agreed that one should not be lenient like him, and this <i>libun</i> was called <i>libun kal</i> (light <i>libun</i>), which is as effective as <i>hagala</i> and even more. When there is a doubt whether a utensil requires <i>libun</i>, many were accustomed to instruct that it be kashered by <i>libun kal</i> (Terumat Ha-deshen 130; Rema OḤ 451:4). A further benefit of <i>libun kal</i> was that it helped to eliminate food remnants that got stuck in the utensil's grooves and were difficult to clean (MB 451:33). And nowadays one can spoil them with soap. The poskim further wrote that there is an even lighter <i>libun</i>, namely when the utensil is heated to a heat of <i>yad soledet bo</i> (Levush; Taz 451:8; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 10). Apparently the intent is 70 degrees and above. And the Pri Megadim (451, Eshel Avraham 30) wrote that it is effective for the absorption of a <i>keli rishon</i> that is not on the fire.<br>Here I wrote that one may rely on the opinion of those who are lenient to perform <i>libun</i> on the pan at the same heat only “in a case of great loss,” whereas regarding Pesaḥ I wrote that one may be lenient “in time of need” (Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ, 5780 edition, 10:4, note 4). This is because regarding Pesaḥ an additional consideration for leniency is joined, namely the opinion of the Rishonim who hold that ḥametz before Pesaḥ is considered <i>heteira bela</i> (“it absorbed a permitted substance”), and it suffices to kasher utensils that absorbed ḥametz through fire by <i>hagala</i> (Peninei Halakha ibid. 10:3). However, regarding forbidden foods, where this consideration does not exist, seemingly one should be stringent in accordance with the opinion of most poskim. Nevertheless, since after a full day it is a dispute concerning a rabbinic law, even though most poskim were stringent, one should rule in a case of great loss according to the lenient opinion. The dispute concerns a rabbinic law because after a full day the flavor stuck to and absorbed in the utensils has become spoiled, and by Torah law these flavors do not render forbidden, and only by rabbinic decree must one perform <i>libun</i> on them to remove the spoiled flavors from them. And even according to the opinion I cited (above 32, note 7), that the kashering itself is required by Torah law and does not depend on the flavor, one may rely on the lenient opinions, since the kashering is in the manner of its absorption. It should further be added that the reasoning of the stringent opinion, that there are flavors that do not come out without severe <i>libun</i>, is correct regarding utensils that absorb, but with metals, which do not truly absorb into their interior, the same level of heat suffices. One may also add the opinion of those who hold that if the pan is greased with oil, the law of the pan is like that of a pot whose absorption is through liquids and whose kashering is by <i>hagala</i> (Shulḥan Gavoha 451:31, and Responsa Tiferet Adam OḤ 16, cited at the end of the next note). The same applies to baking paper."}]},{"slug":"17-33-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-08/","title_he":"ח – מצבי ביניים בין הגעלה לליבון","title_en":"Intermediate Cases Between Hagala and Libun","body_en":"<p>The difference between pots intended for cooking, whose kashering is by <i>hagala</i> in boiling water, and pans and skewers intended for baking and roasting, whose kashering is by <i>libun</i>, is that cooking is done with liquids that soften the dish, and therefore the remnants of the dish stick to and are absorbed in the utensil in a soft manner; whereas the aim of baking and roasting is to reduce the liquids in the foods and harden them, and consequently the food remnants stick to and are absorbed in the utensil in a hard manner. And even though baking pans are greased with oil, their kashering is by <i>libun</i>, since the oil is intended to prevent sticking and not to soften the baked item.</p><p>If non-kosher food that dried out was cooked in a pot, even though at the end of the cooking there was no longer liquid in the pot, its kashering is by <i>hagala</i>, since the beginning of its absorption was through liquids. Moreover, one follows the general purpose of the utensil, which is intended for cooking and not for baking (Rosh).</p><p>Accordingly, a frying pan is kashered by <i>hagala</i>, for the oil put in the frying pan is intended to add moisture to the food. This is the difference between baking and frying: baking dries out the baked item, whereas frying moistens the food. This is the view of most Rishonim (Rosh, Ra'avya, Mordekhai and Sha'arei Dura).</p><p>And some say that a frying pan is kashered by <i>libun</i>, for since people commonly fry in a frying pan even with a little oil, it often happens that the oil runs out and the absorption becomes dry, through fire. Furthermore, sometimes from the start there are places in the frying pan that have no oil, and the absorption there is through fire; therefore the law of the frying pan is like that of a baking pan, and its kashering is by <i>libun</i> (Rashba and Rabbeinu Yoel).</p><p>In practice, one should kasher a frying pan by <i>libun kal</i>, that is, heating the frying pan on the stovetop until, if paper were placed on the frying pan, it would be singed, or if a thin thread were stretched across its rim, it would be singed and tear from the great heat. For in this way its kashering is in the manner of its use. This is also convenient for kashering it, since it involves heating the empty frying pan on the same fire on which one usually fries.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"According to the Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit 4:4), Rabbeinu Yoel and Maharam Ḥalawa, the kashering of a frying pan in which one fried with a little oil is by <i>libun</i>. According to the Ra'avya, Rosh (Pesaḥim 2:7), Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Agur, Shibbolei Ha-leket in the name of the Ge'onim, Mordekhai, Hagahot Maimoniyot and others – by <i>hagala</i>. (According to Rabbeinu Tam, Or Zaru'a, Ra'ah and Ohel Mo'ed, only a utensil that the heat of the fire surrounds on all sides, such as a skewer or a pan inside an oven, requires <i>libun</i>, and according to their view, even if oil was not spread on the bottom of the frying pan – its kashering is by <i>hagala</i>.) The SA YD 121:4 was stringent to kasher a frying pan by <i>libun</i> (and regarding Pesaḥ he was lenient, together with the reasoning of <i>heteira bela</i>; see Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 10, note 3). In practice, I wrote to kasher by <i>libun kal</i>: first, because according to most Rishonim the kashering of a frying pan is by <i>hagala</i>, and all the more so, in their opinion, one may kasher by <i>libun kal</i>, which is more effective than <i>hagala</i>. In addition, some say that the <i>libun</i> must be at the heat of the forbidden use, and if so, even according to those who are stringent to perform <i>libun</i> on a frying pan – <i>libun kal</i> is the <i>libun</i> required for a frying pan (above, note 8). We also learned in note 8 that many ruled that when there is a doubt whether a utensil is kashered by <i>libun</i> or <i>hagala</i>, one may suffice with <i>libun kal</i>. In addition, after a full day from the forbidden use, the flavors that were absorbed and stuck have become spoiled, and then the doubt concerns a rabbinic law, and consequently the halakha follows the lenient opinion that the kashering of a frying pan is by <i>hagala</i>, and so wrote Igrot Moshe (YD 3:14:2). But since in practice it is easy to kasher the frying pan by <i>libun kal</i>, which is in the manner of its regular use, and thereby one satisfies most poskim, this is what I wrote for practice. And according to what was explained above (32, note 8), that the main obligation of kashering is because this is what the Torah taught – to kasher utensils that were used with forbidden substances in the manner of their forbidden use – there is more reason to say that the kashering of a frying pan by <i>libun kal</i> is exactly in the manner of its use.<br>Some say that when there is no sizzling, bubbling oil in the frying pan, the frying pan is considered like a baking pan (Pri Ḥadash 451:11; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 36). On the other hand, some say that when the baking pan is greased with oil, the law of the baking pan is like that of a pot whose absorption is through liquids and whose kashering is by <i>hagala</i> (Shulḥan Gavoha 451:31, and Responsa Tiferet Adam OḤ 16). And it seems in practice, based on the Rosh and those who concur with him, to take the middle path: if the purpose of the oil is to prevent sticking, as is customary when greasing a baking pan with oil, then this is baking and its kashering is by <i>libun</i>; and if the purpose of the oil is to moisten the food and flavor it with oil, as is customary in a frying pan, then this is frying, and the kashering of the frying pan is by <i>hagala</i>."}]},{"slug":"17-33-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-09/","title_he":"ט – תבניות בשר וחלב שהתחלפו","title_en":"Meat and Dairy Baking Pans That Were Mixed Up","body_en":"<p>If a clean meat baking pan was used for a dairy baked item or vice versa, since in practice the meat and dairy flavors did not mix together, the pan can be kashered by <i>hagala</i>, in accordance with the law of <i>heteira bela</i> (explained in the note). All the more so it can be kashered by <i>libun kal</i>, by heating it in the oven at the highest heat for half an hour, for according to some poskim this kashering is considered full <i>libun</i>, which is effective even for absorption of a forbidden substance (as explained in halakha 7).<sup>10</sup></p><p>Likewise, when one wishes to convert a pan from meat to dairy or vice versa, it suffices to kasher it by <i>hagala</i> or by heating it in the oven at the highest heat.</p><p>However, many in Ashkenaz were accustomed not to convert meat utensils to dairy and vice versa by <i>hagala</i>, so that people would not become confused and forget what was dairy and what was meat; but in time of need they were accustomed to be lenient. And in the other communities they ruled that one may perform <i>hagala</i> on a utensil in order to use it for the other kind for any purpose whatsoever, but they were not accustomed to do so frequently, so as not to create confusion in the kitchen between dairy and meat.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"<b><i>Heteira bela</i></b>: What we learned (in halakha 1), that a utensil that absorbed through fire requires kashering through fire, applies on condition that the absorption was of a forbidden substance. For example, a skewer on which non-kosher meat was roasted: since it absorbed forbidden flavor through fire, its kashering is by <i>libun</i>. But if at the time of the absorption the meat was kosher, and only afterwards became forbidden, it can be kashered by <i>hagala</i>. Therefore, a skewer on which sacrificial meat was roasted – after a day, the sacrificial meat becomes <i>notar</i> (leftover sacrificial meat) and forbidden to eat, and the flavor absorbed in the skewer also becomes <i>notar</i>, and therefore the skewer requires kashering. But since at the time the flavor of the meat was absorbed in the skewer the meat was permitted to eat, there is no need to perform <i>libun</i> on it; rather it suffices to kasher it by <i>hagala</i> in boiling water (Avoda Zara 76a). This is because <i>hagala</i> removes almost all the flavors, and what does not come out is only a faint flavor; when the absorption was of a forbidden substance, one must remove even this faint flavor, but when the absorption began with a permitted substance, it suffices to remove the main flavor through <i>hagala</i> (Ramban and Ritva ibid.).<br>Similarly, a baking pan in which a dairy food was baked, and after a full day meat was baked in it – since the dairy flavor was spoiled at the time the meat was baked, in practice dairy and meat flavors did not mix together, and therefore it suffices to kasher the pan by <i>hagala</i>, in accordance with the law of <i>heteira bela</i>. Admittedly, it is forbidden to bake meat in a dairy utensil even if it is not <i>ben yomo</i>; however, since in practice good meat and dairy flavors did not mix, if one did so it is not considered an absorption of a forbidden substance but rather an absorption of a permitted substance that the Sages forbade, and therefore <i>hagala</i> is effective for it. So wrote Issur Ve-heter He-arokh 58:26; Pri Megadim in the introduction to the laws of meat and milk, and others. And according to Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav OḤ 451:13, even baking meat within a full day of baking a dairy food is considered <i>heteira bela</i>, since each time one baked a permitted kind. And if it is a metal pan and it was cleaned between the bakings with soap, as is customary nowadays, according to all opinions, even if the meat was baked in the dairy pan on the same day, its kashering is by <i>hagala</i>, since in practice no remnants of dairy flavor remained in it (above 32:6-8), and this is like <i>heteira bela</i> even according to the Pri Megadim and those who concur with him. In practice, the easy way to kasher the pan is to heat it inside the baking oven at high heat for about half an hour, and according to all opinions this satisfies the requirement of <i>hagala</i>, since <i>libun kal</i> is more effective than <i>hagala</i> (above, note 8).<br>The same law applies to a <i>sir pele</i> and a Teflon frying pan: if they were used with non-kosher food, their kashering is by <i>libun</i>; but if one mistakenly baked or fried dairy in them and after a full day meat, since their absorption is considered “permitted” (<i>heteira</i>), their kashering is by <i>hagala</i>. And with a <i>sir pele</i>, since it is made of metal, if it was cleaned, even if meat was baked in it within a full day, its kashering is by <i>hagala</i>, since a clean metal utensil does not expel flavors. However, if the utensils were not cleaned properly, and they were <i>benei yoman</i>, and one baked or roasted the other kind in them, this is considered <i>issura bela</i> (“it absorbed a forbidden substance”) and their kashering is by <i>libun</i>, as explained in halakha 7."},{"n":11,"en":"The Ashkenazic custom not to perform <i>hagala</i> on utensils to convert them from meat to dairy is cited in Magen Avraham 509:11 in the name of his teachers, and this is so that people will not come to error and forgetfulness as to whether the utensil is meat or dairy. And so wrote many Ashkenazic poskim and a few Sephardic poskim as halakha. However, even according to the stringent custom in Ashkenaz, in a pressing situation, or when the utensil became non-kosher, it is permitted (Pri Megadim 509, Eshel Avraham 11; 452, Eshel Avraham 13). It is also permitted to change the status of the utensil at a time when one is kashering it for Pesaḥ in any case (Ḥatam Sofer YD 110; MB 451:19). It is also permitted to change the utensil after receiving it as a gift or buying it, and some permitted changing it by transferring its ownership to a friend and acquiring it back from him (Darkhei Teshuva YD 121:59). And many wrote that regarding <i>libun</i> there is no custom to be stringent (Maharsham 2:241; Arugot Ha-bosem and others).<br>And many hold that there is no such restriction at all, and it is permitted <i>le-khatḥila</i> to perform <i>hagala</i> on a utensil in order to change its use, for so it emerges from all the Rishonim, and so wrote Pri Ḥadash YD 97:1; Pri To'ar 97:1; Sedei Ḥemed; Yabi'a Omer vol. 3 YD 4. This is also the custom of those of Sephardic origin. However, apparently even in Sephardic communities they were not accustomed to change the use frequently, for it is clear that frequent change is liable to cause confusion. And so wrote Rabbi Shlomo Kimḥi of Izmir in “Yemei Shlomo” on Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Assurot 17:3; and in our time, Divrei David vol. 1 YD 12:5. In addition, the Ḥida wrote in Maḥazik Berakha 509:2 that when the utensil is <i>ben yomo</i>, one should not perform <i>hagala</i> on it in order to change it."}]},{"slug":"17-33-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-10/","title_he":"י – מדריך להכשרת מטבח טרף","title_en":"A Guide to Kashering a Non-Kosher Kitchen","body_en":"<p><b>Sink and countertop</b>: One must clean them well, paying attention to the grooves, so that no food residue remains in them. Afterwards one must pour boiling water over the sink and its walls and over the entire countertop surface. Before the pouring, one must make sure the sink and countertop are dry, so that the boiling water will be poured directly onto them and not be cooled by the water on them. In a home kitchen it is customary to kasher the sink and countertop with water heated in an electric kettle, and since the water in the kettle is not enough to kasher the sinks and the whole countertop, the kashering is carried out in stages, where at each stage one pours the water boiled in the kettle over another area; and if the poured water flowed to a part that has not yet been kashered, one must sweep it away from there before pouring additional water boiled in the kettle over it. Therefore, one who wishes to save time in the kashering process should first pour over the bottom of the sink, then over its four walls, then begin to kasher the places adjacent to the sink, and from there continue gradually to the more distant places. Cleaning with a high-quality steam cleaner is as effective as pouring.</p><p>A delicate countertop that is liable to be damaged by pouring boiling water is kashered by rinsing and good cleaning (Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 11:1-2).</p><p><b>Stovetop</b>: <i>Le-khatḥila</i>, one should clean the stovetop and perform <i>libun kal</i> on the grate. When it is difficult to perform <i>libun</i> on the grate, one may suffice with cleaning the grate and performing <i>hagala</i> on it in boiling water; and when the grate is longer than the depth of the pot, one should first insert one side and afterwards the other side. The other iron parts of the grate that do not touch the pots, as well as the enamel surface under the grate and the gas burner heads, must be cleaned well of all food residue, but since these parts do not come into contact with the pots, there is no need to perform <i>libun</i> or <i>hagala</i> on them. <i>Le-khatḥila</i>, it is good to light all the burners for about a quarter of an hour (see Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 11:2, note 2).</p><p><b>Electric stovetop</b>: One cleans it well and heats it at high heat for about a quarter of an hour, and <i>ke-bol'o kakh polto</i>. The same law applies to a <b>ceramic stovetop</b>, which looks like opaque glass, on which the pots are placed directly. <b>Induction stovetop</b>: One cleans the surface and pours boiling water over it, and heats pots on it for about a quarter of an hour, so that they will heat the surface under them in the manner of their use (see above 25, note 14).</p><p><b>Cutlery and pots</b>: One first cleans them, fills a large pot with water, brings it to a boil and mixes soapy water into it in order to spoil its taste. While the water is boiling, one puts the cutlery into it, one utensil after another, and thereby kashers the cutlery. Likewise, one puts in the small pots that can fit into it, and thereby kashers them. The kashering of the large pot and of pots that do not fit into it was explained in halakha 4.</p><p><b>Baking oven</b>: One cleans the oven of food remnants and operates it at the highest heat for about half an hour, and thereby the body of the oven is kashered. <b>Baking pans</b> are not kashered, since according to most poskim they must be kashered by severe <i>libun</i>, and this is liable to damage them. In a case of great loss, for example when there is no way to obtain new pans or their price is high, they may be kashered by heating them in the oven at the highest heat for about half an hour (above halakha 7 and note 8).</p><p><b>Microwave</b>: It should be kashered in three stages: a) one cleans the food residue that may have remained in it due to boiling over and splatters; b) one performs <i>hagala</i> on the rotating plate in boiling water; c) one places in it a small dish of water with soap and heats it for about ten minutes at the highest heat, and thereby kashers it from the vapors and condensation that stuck to and were absorbed in it during its use with non-kosher food.</p><p><b>Dishwasher</b>: One cleans the filter of the food remnants in it and runs the dishwasher at the highest heat, and thereby kashers the dishwasher and the racks together (see Peninei Halakha: Pesaḥ 11, note 5).</p><p><b>Table</b>: If it is delicate and boiling pots are not placed on it, it suffices to clean it well with soap and water. If it is not delicate and boiling pots are sometimes placed on it, one should clean it and afterwards pour boiling water over it.</p><p><b>Refrigerator and cabinets</b>: They are kashered by thorough cleaning with soap and water, in order to remove any sauce residue that may have stuck to them.</p><p><b>Mixer and blender</b>: If they were used with cold foods, they are kashered by cleaning with soap and water alone. If they were used with boiling foods, they are kashered by <i>hagala</i>.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-33-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-33-11/","title_he":"יא – שימוש במטבח ובמנגל טרף","title_en":"Using a Non-Kosher Kitchen and Barbecue","body_en":"<p>As we have learned (32:3), it is forbidden to use utensils that require kashering for hot foods, for since forbidden foods were cooked in them, the Torah commanded that they be kashered. Now we will add that <i>le-khatḥila</i> one also should not use them for cold foods, even on an occasional basis. And one also should not use meat utensils for cold dairy foods or vice versa.</p><p>However, in a pressing situation, such as one who has arrived at a guesthouse (<i>tzimmer</i>) and has no kosher utensils and no possibility of kashering the non-kosher utensils, he may occasionally use clean meat utensils for cold dairy foods or vice versa. Likewise, in a pressing situation it is permitted to use clean non-kosher utensils for cold foods, dry and liquid, such as plates for salad, cups for cold drinks, teaspoons for eating yogurt and cheese, and soup bowls for milk with cereal. This is because as long as the utensils are clean and one eats cold foods in them, they do not cause flavors to be absorbed into the foods.<sup>12</sup></p><p>When one wishes to use a barbecue grill, since the food touches the grate directly, one must kasher it. And since its use is in fire, its kashering is also in fire, and one must make sure that the fire reaches all parts of the grate, as it could have reached them when it was used with non-kosher food. There is no need to clean the grate, for since it is kashered by severe <i>libun</i>, all the foods stuck to it are burned and become completely unfit to be food.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Many Rishonim hold that it is permitted to use a non-kosher utensil for cold foods, since cold foods do not absorb the flavor absorbed in and stuck to the non-kosher utensil. So wrote the Terumah, Semag, Semak, Rosh, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Orḥot Ḥayim, Ohel Mo'ed, Sha'arei Dura, Maharil and SA 94:3. On the other hand, some say that it is forbidden to use a non-kosher utensil even with cold foods, lest one use it with hot foods (Roke'aḥ and Mordekhai). And some say that only dry cold foods may be eaten in a non-kosher utensil, but not liquids (Tosafot, Issur Ve-heter He-arokh, Radbaz). The Rema 121:5 ruled that one should not use non-kosher utensils on a regular basis, but on an occasional basis it is permitted. And so wrote many Aḥaronim, among them: Shakh 91, end of sub-paragraph 3; Pri Ḥadash 3; Pri Megadim. And although it seems at first glance from the SA that he permitted <i>le-khatḥila</i> using non-kosher utensils even on a regular basis for cold foods, it is clear that he was lenient in this only <i>be-di'avad</i> and in time of need, for why would he permit using non-kosher utensils <i>le-khatḥila</i>?<br>There is another problem with these utensils: presumably they were not immersed, even though they were manufactured by gentiles, and we have already learned (above 31:8) that according to most poskim it is rabbinically forbidden to eat from utensils that were not immersed, even on an occasional basis. However, since these are utensils intended for hosting in a hotel or restaurant, and some say that they need not be immersed because they are not intended for eating but for commerce, in a pressing situation one may use them. Therefore I wrote above that only in a pressing situation may one use them. However, if the owner of the guesthouse is a gentile who is not obligated to immerse his utensils, and the entire problem is only that the utensils are non-kosher, then even in a time of need that is not a pressing situation one may be lenient and use his utensils for cold foods.<br>Utensils that have no way of being kashered, such as earthenware utensils that became non-kosher, may not be used even on an occasional basis (Ramban, Ran, Ritva, Meiri, Maggid Mishneh, Shakh 91:3; Pri Ḥadash 92:1; Pri To'ar 91:2). The permission to use, in time of need and in a pressing situation, a meat utensil for cold milk or vice versa is explained above 25:9, note 9."}]}]});
